---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const P1 = `i ← 1
REPEAT UNTIL(i > n)
{
  j ← 1
  REPEAT UNTIL(j > n)
  {
    <one step>
    j ← j + 1
  }
  i ← i + 1
}`;
const P2 = `left ← n
steps ← 0

REPEAT UNTIL(left < 1)
{
  steps ← steps + 1
  left ← (left - (left MOD 2)) / 2
}

DISPLAY(steps)`;
const P3 = `i ← 1
REPEAT UNTIL(i > n)
{
  j ← 1
  REPEAT UNTIL(j > 3)
  {
    <one step>
    j ← j + 1
  }
  i ← i + 1
}`;
const RefCard = `REPEAT n TIMES { }       runs the block n times
                         NO loop counter is provided

REPEAT UNTIL(condition)  repeats UNTIL it becomes true
                         keep your own counter if you need one

a ← expression            assign a COPY of the result
a MOD b                  remainder.  17 MOD 5 is 2
a / b                    TRUE division.  17 / 5 is 3.4

There is no for loop on the reference sheet.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Pseudocode Challenge: Counting the Steps</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Three loops. For each one, count how many times the marked statement
            runs, for <b>n = 8, then 16, then 32</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Do not run these. Count them.</b> The written-response section
            asks exactly this about your own program: state how many times the
            body of the loop executes. There is no computer in the room for
            that either.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Do this on paper.</b> There is nothing to run. On exam day you
            get this notation printed and no computer.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="reference">
          <h2>Reference Card</h2>

          <Code code={RefCard} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Exam Notation — what you have so far" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="problems">
          <h2>The Problems</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 1 &mdash; count</h3>

          <p>How many times does <code>&lt;one step&gt;</code> run, for n = 8, 16, and 32?</p>

          <Code code={P1} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Problem 1" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <p>
            <b>Then answer this:</b> the input doubled twice. What happened to
            the step count each time? Name the shape.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 2 &mdash; count</h3>

          <p>What is displayed, for n = 8, 16, and 32?</p>

          <Code code={P2} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Problem 2" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <p>
            <b>Then answer this:</b> you have seen these three numbers before.
            Which algorithm from this unit do they belong to, and why is that not
            a coincidence?
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Problem 3 &mdash; count</h3>

          <p>
            This looks like Problem 1. How many times does{" "}
            <code>&lt;one step&gt;</code> run, for n = 8, 16, and 32?
          </p>

          <Code code={P3} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Problem 3" showLineNumbers={false} class="mb-3" />

          <p>
            <b>Then answer this:</b> Problem 1 and Problem 3 are both a loop
            inside a loop, and only one of them grows like n squared. Say what in
            the code decides that, in one sentence.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And this:</b> at n = 8 this problem does more steps than Problem 1
            does. At n = 32 it does far fewer. Explain why comparing two
            algorithms at one small size is a bad idea.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your nine step counts, laid out in a table with a row per problem and
            a column per value of n. Plus the written answers.
          </p>

          <p>It is collected as <b>Homework Drop 8</b>, together with Question Set 12 and Pseudocode Challenge: Tracing a Sort. <b>The drop is worth 4 points</b> for everything in it, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
